      "id": 2,
      "claim": "A decrease in stream velocity results in a reduction of both the stream's competence and capacity.",
      "evidence": "Lower velocity reduces kinetic energy and shear stress, meaning the water can no longer support the same size or amount of sediment in suspension or bedload.",
      "weight": 0.25,
      "depends_on": [1]
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "claim": "When the sediment load exceeds the reduced transport capacity, particles settle out of the flow.",
      "evidence": "Deposition occurs when the flow velocity falls below the critical settling velocity of the particles, causing them to drop onto the stream bed.",
      "weight": 0.25,
      "depends_on": [2]
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "claim": "Therefore, the deposition of material in the stream increases as velocity decreases.",
      "evidence": "Direct logical consequence of step 3: reduced capacity leads to increased settling/deposition.",
      "weight": 0.25,
